GOAL (increment 1, the last open pinned gap): an `atLeast(field=selections, scope=force)` condition whose `childId` names the root `entryLink` 9e50-7486-65ab-c449 must also count the roster selection that carries only the link's resolved target id (entryId 8923-5946-7b10-8957, entryLinkId empty), so the `set 1` modifier on Greasus's constraint cef8-c3b1-7850-85bc applies and the slot reports effectiveMax 1.

WHY IT FAILS TODAY (read, not run). `rosterFromRos` binds a selection under `entryLinkId || entryId` (src/evaluator/__fixtures__/rosParser.js:38), so the toggle selection binds under 8923; `attachInstance` looks that id up (`resolved.lookup`) and the node's `def` is the .gst shared `selectionEntry` 8923 (kind ENTRY, no `targetId`). `targetsOf` (src/evaluator/countIndex.js:132-143) therefore indexes it under `null`, its own id 8923, its effective category ids and its raw type `upgrade` — never under 9e50. The condition asks the index for key `(forceFrame, 9e50)` and gets 0, so the `set 1` never fires and the written max 0 stands. The index already covers the mirror direction (a link node counts under `def.targetId`, countIndex.js:136), which is exactly the asymmetry the pin exposes.

THE RULE (docs/battlescribe-data-format.md §9.7, 'Zwei Fallstricke'): "Der `childId` von `<repeat>`/`condition` kann die **Ziel-ID** (`entryLink.targetId`) *oder* die lokale Link-ID sein — beim Zählen beide Fälle über `resolveEntry` abgleichen." The engine already applies the same reasoning one layer over, for constraint anchors: src/evaluator/constraints.js:117 counts `isLinkDefinition(node.def) ? node.def.targetId : node.def.id`, with the comment that counting the link's own id would drop every selection that came in through another door to the same target. Conditions are the one query kind that still counts the raw written id.

THE FIX (one place, static, in the layer that already normalizes conditions). In src/evaluator/resolver.js add a sibling of `normalizeForceInstanceCondition` — e.g. `normalizeLinkChildId(condition, byId)` — and call it from `resolveConditionWitnesses` (resolver.js:417-425) per condition, AFTER `normalizeForceInstanceCondition` and BEFORE `resolveConditionWitness`:

```js
function normalizeLinkChildId(condition, byId) {
  const childId = condition.targetChildId;
  if (childId === null || childId === undefined || childId === '') return;
  const definition = byId.get(childId);
  if (definition?.kind !== DefinitionKind.ENTRY_LINK) return;
  const { targetId } = definition;
  if (targetId === null || targetId === undefined || targetId === '') return;
  condition.targetChildId = targetId;
}
```

Give it a JSDoc block in the house style (German, like its neighbours): state the §9.7 rule, name constraints.js:117 as the same rule one layer over, and say that the index counts a link node under both its own id and its `targetId`, so rewriting the written link id to the target id is a superset match and never loses the link-bound direction.

ORDERING CONSTRAINT — DO NOT USE `definition.resolved`. `resolveModifierTargets` (and with it `resolveConditionWitnesses`) runs at resolver.js:700, BEFORE the `entryLink` resolution loop at resolver.js:708 sets `link.resolved`. Only the parsed `targetId` attribute is available at that point. That is also the semantically right choice: `targetsOf` indexes a link node under its immediate `targetId`, so normalizing one step keeps both directions matched. A link→link chain would need `resolved.id` on both sides (childId normalization AND `targetsOf`); I measured the frozen fixture set and there is no such chain — of the 26 distinct condition/repeat `childId`s that name an `entryLink`, 0 point at another link. Do not add the transitive variant in this increment.

SCOPE OF THE REWRITE, and what is deliberately left alone:
- CONDITIONS ONLY. `targetChildId` exists on conditions and on repeats (catalogReader.js:464 and :562), and §9.7 names both. I measured the split in the fixture catalogues: 22 distinct link-naming `childId`s sit on conditions (68 occurrences overall), 5 on repeats — among them the two links of the pinned `dispel-scroll-repeat-group-max` scenario. Normalizing repeats too would change repeat counts in a scenario nothing in this increment asks about, so leave `repeat.targetChildId` untouched. Say so in the JSDoc; a later issue can extend it.
- NO RESTRICTION TO ROOT LINKS. The doc states the rule for any link, and 16 of the 22 link-naming condition `childId`s in the fixture data are nested links. Restricting to root `entryLink`s is the fallback if — and only if — the suite shows a sibling scenario regressing (see below), not the first attempt.
- REJECTED: adding the reverse alias (entry counts under the ids of the links pointing at it) in `countIndex.targetsOf`. It needs a reverse index threaded from the resolver through `buildEvalTree`/`buildIndex`, and it would make every entry count under every link id in the dataset, across catalogues.
- REJECTED: normalizing inside `query()`. The query primitive has no definition lookup in its context; adding one means touching `createQueryContext`, constraints.js and modifiers.js for a mapping that is static and roster-independent — exactly what the resolver layer exists for (resolver.js:20-26).
- REJECTED: binding the roster selection to the link definition instead of the target in `attachInstance`. It changes node identity (`def.id`) and with it slot `defId`s, offer dedup, phantom absence checks and report source ids — far past this increment.

KNOWN SIDE EFFECTS the implementer must expect (all inside the check command):
1. `condition.witnessDefinition` (resolver.js:402-408) now resolves to the target ENTRY instead of the link. That is still a valid witness kind (`WITNESS_DEFINITION_KINDS`), and it is the better answer — the witness names the selection the user actually made. Only one scenario asserts a witness at all (docs/testing/violation-classification, `witnessDefId` 4e15-0353-165f-5528 'Border Patrols rules'), and that id is not an `entryLink`, so it is unaffected.
2. Six other pinned scenarios hold a roster that carries an id that is the target of some link-naming condition `childId`: author-message-severity (8923), condition-group-not (7d87), dispel-scroll-repeat-group-max (b76c — via repeats only, so untouched), modifier-characteristic-value (8b76), unit-scope-per-model-cost (50e2/8649/dde4), unlimited-modifier-toggle (50e2/8b76). Whether a count actually changes there depends on the dataset each scenario loads. The full `npx vitest run src/evaluator` is the judge.
3. In the pinned scenario's own catalogue two further `set 1` modifiers gate on 9e50 (Ogre Kingdoms .cat lines 1046 and 1353, for other special characters). With the toggle selected their max-0 constraints also become max 1; neither character is in either roster, so no violation appears or disappears from what the scenario asserts.

IF A SIBLING SCENARIO GOES RED: do not touch anything under docs/testing/ — the scenarios are the specification (issue 'Out of scope'). Narrow the rewrite instead (root `entryLink`s only, i.e. links reachable directly under a catalogue/gameSystem root), re-run, and report in your step return which scenario forced the narrowing and with what numbers.

EXPECTED RESULT for the pinned case, traced through the code: with the fix the toggle node contributes to key `(forceFrame, 8923)` in the BASE bucket, `query` with includeChildSelections=true reads 1 >= 1, the `set 1` rewrites cef8-c3b1-7850-85bc to 1, the Greasus slot reports effectiveMax 1, headroom 0, and the constraint no longer fires. Roster 01 keeps counting 0 and stays exactly as it is.
